  "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)\u2248f(a,b,c)+\u2207f(a,b,c)\\cdot \u27e8x-a,y-b,z-c\u27e9.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & \u2248f(-1,0,1)+\u2207f(-1,0,1)\\cdot \u27e8x-(-1),y-0,z-1\u27e9 \\\\  & \u2248-1+\u27e8{f}_{x}(-1,0,1),{f}_{y}(-1,0,1),{f}_{z}(-1,0,1)\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8x+1,y,z-1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(-1,0,1)$, ${f}_{y}(-1,0,1)$ and ${f}_{z}(-1,0,1),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-1,0,1) & ={1}^{2}=1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(-1,0,1) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{z}(-1,0,1) & =2(-1)(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)\u2248-1+\u27e81,1,-2\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8x+1,y,z-1\u27e9.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(-0.95,0.02,1.04)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-0.95,y=0.02$ and $y=1.04,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-0.95,0.02,1.04) & \u2248-1+\u27e81,1,-2\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8-0.95+1,0.02,1.04-1\u27e9 \\\\  & \u2248-1+\u27e81,1,-2\u27e9\\cdot \u27e80.05,0.02,0.04\u27e9 \\\\  & \u2248-1+1(0.05)+1(0.02)-2(0.04) \\\\  & \u2248-1+0.05+0.02-0.08 \\\\  & \u2248-1.01\\end{aligned}\n$$",
